          "imageUrl": "",
          "explanation": ""
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "text": "√(1 + x²)",
          "isCorrect": false,
          "index": 3,
          "imageUrl": "",
          "explanation": ""
        }
      ],
      "answer": "",
      "alternateAnswers": [],
      "explanation": "Let θ = sin⁻¹(x). This means sin(θ) = x.\nSince 0 < x < 1, θ lies in (0, π/2), which is in the first quadrant.\nIn the first quadrant, cos(θ) is positive.\nWe know the identity sin²(θ) + cos²(θ) = 1.\nSo, cos²(θ) = 1 - sin²(θ) = 1 - x².\nTherefore, cos(θ) = √(1 - x²) (taking the positive root as θ is in the first quadrant).\nThus, cos(sin⁻¹(x)) = √(1 - x²).",
