      "explanation": "**Assertion (A):** Let y = tan⁻¹( (3x - x³) / (1 - 3x²) ).\nWe know the identity 3tan⁻¹(x) = tan⁻¹( (3x - x³) / (1 - 3x²) ), which is valid for -1/√3 < x < 1/√3.\nSo, y = 3tan⁻¹(x).\nDifferentiating with respect to x: dy/dx = 3 * (1 / (1 + x²)) = 3 / (1 + x²).\nThus, Assertion A is true.\n\n**Reason (R):** The formula 3tan⁻¹(x) = tan⁻¹( (3x - x³) / (1 - 3x²) ) is *not* valid for all real x. Its validity condition is -1/√3 < x < 1/√3. If x is outside this range, the identity does not hold, or the principal value branch might be different.\nThus, Reason R is false.\nTherefore, A is true but R is false.",
