      "explanation": "We know the identity sin⁻¹(x) + cos⁻¹(x) = π/2.\nSimilarly, sin⁻¹(y) + cos⁻¹(y) = π/2.\n\nGiven: sin⁻¹(x) + sin⁻¹(y) = 2π/3.\nWe can rewrite sin⁻¹(x) as (π/2 - cos⁻¹(x)) and sin⁻¹(y) as (π/2 - cos⁻¹(y)).\nSubstitute these into the given equation:\n(π/2 - cos⁻¹(x)) + (π/2 - cos⁻¹(y)) = 2π/3\nπ - (cos⁻¹(x) + cos⁻¹(y)) = 2π/3\ncos⁻¹(x) + cos⁻¹(y) = π - 2π/3\ncos⁻¹(x) + cos⁻¹(y) = (3π - 2π) / 3\ncos⁻¹(x) + cos⁻¹(y) = π/3.",
